					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-6415246#post-4018194</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-6415246/how-do-i-delete-articles#post-4018194</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Mon, 08 Oct 2018 22:56:57 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>The Great Hippo</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>3938622</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>I think you and I have disagreed over this point before (in regards to the popularity of bad writing &#8212; see the original SCP-049), so I'll try not to bore you <em>too much</em> by hashing over the same old arguments:</p> <p>I don't think it's wrong for you to take issue with this article, but I also don't think it's wrong for someone to enjoy this. The article invokes two very specific ideas: 1) It's very dumb and 2) It's <em>authentically</em> dumb. I think that last point is important, and why this doesn't bother me. I'd likely feel much differently if the author had intended it to succeed.</p> <p>I get someone hating the idea of what this article represents, or the notion of enshrining dumb things on the site &#8212; especially when they put <em>so much effort</em> into their own work (god, I get that). But I also very much believe in the importance of celebrating dumb things. If <em>nothing</em> else, they deflate our pretentiousness: They remind us that this is a fanfic website about a poopy murder statue.</p> <p><strong>tl;dr:</strong> I get what you're saying and I don't think you're wrong, but I also think it's fucking great when the guy in the art museum asking where the toilet is gets mistaken for performance art. Because, as an artist, I love being reminded that what I love most (the art of writing) is, at its core, <em>kind of dumb</em>.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
